180: % ======================================================================
181: \section{Research and publication strategy}
182: \label{sec:pesquisa}
183: % ======================================================================
184: 
185: \subsection{The gap in the literature}
186: 
187: The review covered three fronts. \textbf{(i)}~Classical compass-needle arrays: the founding work (square $22\times22$ lattice, Phys.\ Rev.\ B \textbf{58}, 9238, 1998~\cite{prb1998}) uses equilibrium Monte Carlo with a random field mimicking thermal noise, not Newtonian dynamics. \textbf{(ii)}~Classical dipoles in honeycomb/kagome/triangular lattices (2012--2025): Luttinger--Tisza methods, energy minimization, spin waves, and Monte Carlo with \emph{parallel tempering}, all focused on ground states and thermal \emph{equilibrium} transitions. \textbf{(iii)}~\emph{Artificial spin ice}~\cite{nisoli2013}: real temporal dynamics is studied, but through discrete macrospins (Ising-like) or experimental data (MOKE/MFM), almost never with continuous angular dynamics and explicit mechanical inertia.
188: 
189: The question of how damping controls hysteresis and avalanches is active and publishable in adjacent fields. It is established that underdamped and overdamped regimes form different universality classes in short-range or mean-field systems~\cite{dahmen2011}, but this has not been answered for complete dipolar coupling (long-range, anisotropic) with real spatial geometry. This is the gap occupied by the project.
190: 
191: \subsection{Study design and decision criterion}
192: 
193: The central study sweeps damping over $\sim 2.5$ orders of magnitude in $Q$ (from $Q \approx 15$, underdamped, to $Q \approx 0.05$, overdamped), with multiple initial-noise seeds in the three geometries. It measures hysteresis-loop area, coercive field, remanence, avalanche statistics (power-law exponent $\tau$ in $P(s) \sim s^{-\tau}$, fitted by maximum likelihood using the Clauset--Shalizi--Newman method~\cite{clauset2009}), and domain-size distribution in free relaxation.
194: 
195: The decision criterion was defined \emph{a priori}:
196: \begin{itemize}
197: \item \textbf{Result A (incremental, solid)}: $\tau$ approximately constant with $Q$ (only scales/cutoffs change), consistent with known phenomenology and publishable in the standard target journals;
198: \item \textbf{Result B (surprising)}: $\tau$ varies systematically with $Q$, or there is a qualitative difference between geometries, signaling a breakdown of universality due to anisotropic long-range dipolar coupling. This would justify attempting PRL or a similar venue.
199: \end{itemize}
200: 
201: \subsection{Target journals and APC funding}
202: 
203: \begin{itemize}
204: \item \textbf{CAPES--ACS agreement}: it was confirmed that the CAPES transformative \emph{Read \& Publish} agreement covers \textbf{100\% of the APC in any ACS journal} (CC~BY license) for corresponding authors from consortium institutions, with eligibility checked during submission via ORCID, removing cost as a decision factor;
205: \item \textbf{Primary target}: \emph{ACS Materials Au} (IF~6.5, Q2), the highest-impact fully \emph{open access} ACS option. The paper must be framed as a model for \emph{artificial spin ice} and patterned nanomagnets, which was incorporated into the introduction design;
206: \item \textbf{Backup target}: \emph{ACS Physical Chemistry Au} (IF~4.3, Q2), whose scope explicitly includes condensed matter and is safer in fit;
207: \item \textbf{High-impact alternatives}: \emph{Physical Review Letters} is \emph{outside} the CAPES agreement (APS is not a participating publisher); \emph{Nature Communications}, being \emph{gold OA}, is excluded from \emph{Read \& Publish} agreements (APC $\sim$ USD~6,190 separately). Both are justified only for a type-B result;
208: \item \textbf{Other CAPES publishers} (Springer Nature $\sim$1,750 hybrid titles, Elsevier $\sim$160, Wiley, IEEE, RSP, ACM): a partial review did not find, up to letter~H of the Springer list, a title with better fit than the ACS targets; natural Elsevier candidates (\emph{Physica A}, \emph{JMMM}) await verification in the eligible-title list.
209: \end{itemize}
210: 
211: % ======================================================================
212: \section{Campaign infrastructure and pilot study}
213: \label{sec:campanha}
214: % ======================================================================
215: 
216: \subsection{The two scripts}
217: 
218: \textbf{\code{damping\_sweep.py}} orchestrates the campaign by calling the simulator programmatically without modifying the source code. It builds the log-spaced damping grid in $Q$ from the real physics (inertia and moment derived from geometry), runs Step~1 (hysteresis) and Step~2 (free relaxation with domain statistics) for each geometry~$\times$~$Q$~$\times$~seed combination, and saves per-run CSVs, JSON metadata (including $Q$, $\omega_0$, $B_{\mathrm{ref}}$, domain distribution), and a consolidated \code{manifest.csv}.
219: 
220: \textbf{\code{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}} reads \code{manifest.csv} and produces: loop area (closed-line integral), coercivity and remanence (interpolation at zero crossings); avalanche detection in $dM/dB$ within each monotonic ramp segment (robust threshold of $4\times$MAD); power-law fitting by MLE with $x_{\min}$ selected by KS distance~\cite{clauset2009}, \emph{aggregating avalanches from all seeds} for each $(geometry, Q)$ point before fitting, a methodological choice required for reliable statistics; and final plots, including the central $\tau(Q)$ test by geometry.
221: 
222: Validations performed: area against an exact rectangular loop; $B_c$/$M_r$ against a synthetic loop with known values; avalanche detection against injected steps (size recovery with $\sim$1\% error); MLE against a synthetic sample with exact $\tau = 2.5$ (recovered $2.516 \pm 0.021$), through both code paths (the \code{powerlaw} package, with a real bug in the installed version corrected, and the in-house fallback implementation, with identical results).
223: 
224: \subsection{Pilot-study results}
225: 
226: A reduced pilot (array $14\times14 = 196$ needles, 5 $Q$ values, 3 seeds, 3 geometries, shortened cycle) validated the full pipeline and already displays the central signal sought (Figure~\ref{fig:tau} and Table~\ref{tab:piloto}): the exponent $\tau$ \textbf{increases systematically with $Q$} in all three geometries, from $\tau \approx 1.2$--$1.3$ in the \emph{crossover} ($Q \approx 0.87$) to $\tau \approx 1.7$--$1.8$ in the underdamped regime ($Q = 15$). For the two most overdamped $Q$ values, the shortened pilot cycle does not generate enough avalanches for fitting, which is the correct pipeline behavior: it reports unavailability instead of a spurious number.
227: 
228: \begin{table}[h]
229: \centering
230: \small
231: \caption{Pilot: avalanche exponent $\tau$ (MLE, avalanches aggregated across 3 seeds) by geometry and quality factor. ``---'' indicates insufficient statistics ($n_{\mathrm{tail}} <$ minimum) in the shortened pilot cycle.}
232: \label{tab:piloto}
233: \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
234: \toprule
235: $Q$ & square & triangular & honeycomb \\
236: \midrule
237: 15.0  & $1.81 \pm 0.15$ & $1.69 \pm 0.13$ & $1.73 \pm 0.15$ \\
238: 3.60  & $1.62 \pm 0.10$ & $1.51 \pm 0.08$ & $1.60 \pm 0.12$ \\
239: 0.866 & $1.19 \pm 0.04$ & $1.29 \pm 0.06$ & --- \\
240: 0.208 & --- & --- & --- \\
241: 0.050 & --- & --- & --- \\
242: \bottomrule
243: \end{tabular}
244: \end{table}
245: 
246: \begin{figure}[t]
247: \centering
248: \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figs/tau_vs_Q.png}
249: \caption{Central test of the study (pilot): avalanche power-law exponent $\tau$ as a function of the quality factor $Q$, by geometry. The systematic increase of $\tau$ with $Q$, if confirmed in the full campaign with adequate statistics, is the candidate signature that the universality class depends on the damping regime under dipolar coupling.}
250: \label{fig:tau}
251: \end{figure}
252: 
253: \begin{figure}[t]
254: \centering
255: \includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figs/area_vs_Q.png}
256: \caption{Pilot: hysteresis-loop area as a function of $Q$, by geometry. The area increases monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (more dissipation per cycle), with the three geometries qualitatively similar in this metric, unlike domain size, for which the triangular lattice shows non-monotonic behavior to be investigated.}
257: \label{fig:area}
258: \end{figure}
259: 
260: Two secondary findings from the pilot are worth recording: (i) loop area and coercive field increase monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (Figure~\ref{fig:area}); (ii) the mean domain size in the \emph{triangular} lattice shows non-monotonicity with $Q$ that is not seen in the other geometries, exactly the kind of frustration~$\times$~inertia interaction identified in the study design as a type-B candidate result. Both require the full campaign for confirmation.
261: 
262: \subsection{Full campaign (ready to run)}
263: 
264: Defined parameters: $30\times30$ lattice (900 needles), 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\,15]$, 5 seeds, 3 geometries $\Rightarrow$ 120 hysteresis runs + 120 relaxation runs. Cost with V39 code: $\sim$2--2.5~min per run ($\approx$~7--8~h serial campaign). \textbf{With V40 optimization} (precomputed tensor, Section~\ref{sec:melhorias}): $\sim$3.3--3.4~s per run $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{$\sim$15~min serial campaign on a single core}, and only a few minutes with \code{--n\_workers} on the \code{jps@mumax} server, with no GPU required.
265: 
266: % ======================================================================
267: \section{Step-by-step roadmap for the proposed studies}
268: \label{sec:roteiro}
269: % ======================================================================
270: 
271: This section details, in operational format, the four studies identified as publication candidates, from the central study to the satellites. For each study it specifies the question, simulation steps, analysis, expected result, and editorial destination. All use only already implemented capabilities (V39 + campaign scripts), except where indicated.
272: 
273: \subsection{Study 1 (central): damping as a control parameter for hysteresis and avalanches}
274: 
275: \textbf{Question.} Does the quality factor $Q$ control the universality class of avalanches and the shape of the hysteresis loop under complete dipolar coupling? Does the answer depend on geometry?
276: 
277: \begin{enumerate}
278: \item \textbf{Calibration} ($\sim$30~min): isolated needle in pulse mode; fit the free-decay envelope and validate that the measured $Q$ agrees with $Q = \omega_0 I / b$. This ensures that the damping grid actually spans both regimes;
279: \item \textbf{Hysteresis campaign}: $30\times30$ array; 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\,15]$; 5 seeds; 3 geometries; five-ramp cycle with $B_{\max} = 8\,B_{\mathrm{ref}}$ and duration $\propto T_0$ (120 runs, $\sim$4.5~h on serial CPU without V40);
280: \item \textbf{Relaxation campaign}: same combinations with $B = 0$, starting from the same noise, up to the $S$ plateau; domain statistics in the final state (120 runs, $\sim$3~h without V40);
281: \item \textbf{Analysis} (\code{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}): area, $B_c$, and $M_r$ vs.\ $Q$ by geometry; $\tau(Q)$ by MLE with avalanches aggregated across seeds; domain size/number vs.\ $Q$;
282: \item \textbf{Robustness} (mandatory before conclusions): convergence in $\Delta t$ at the extremes of $Q$; sizes $N = 20$ and $40$ at $Q \approx 1$; comparison with/without PBC;
283: \item \textbf{Decision and writing}: A/B criterion from Section~\ref{sec:pesquisa}; framing as a ``model for \emph{artificial spin ice} / patterned nanomagnets'' in the introduction.
284: \end{enumerate}
285: 
286: \textbf{Expected result.} The pilot suggests increasing $\tau$ with $Q$ in the three geometries. If confirmed with full statistics and surviving robustness diagnostics, this is a type-B candidate result. \textbf{Destination:} ACS Materials Au (result A) or an attempt at PRL (result B), with ACS Physical Chemistry Au as backup.
287: 
288: \subsection{Study 2 (satellite): geometric frustration and domain-formation kinetics}
289: 
290: \textbf{Question.} Does geometric frustration (triangular $>$ honeycomb $>$ square) accelerate or slow down domain coarsening? Is the non-monotonicity of domain size with $Q$ seen in the triangular-lattice pilot real?
291: 
292: \begin{enumerate}
293: \item Long free relaxation ($B = 0$) in the three geometries, same seeds, for 3--4 representative $Q$ values;
294: \item Record $S(t)$ and label domains at intermediate times. This \emph{requires minor instrumentation}: domain statistics are currently computed only in the final state; periodic labeling \emph{snapshots} must be added along the trajectory;
295: \item Extract the time to the $S$ plateau and the growth law of the characteristic domain size $L(t) \sim t^{n}$, comparing exponent $n$ across geometries and damping regimes.
296: \end{enumerate}
297: 
298: \textbf{Destination:} may form part of the central article (domain section) or, if the frustration~$\times$~inertia interaction is rich, a separate article in ACS Physical Chemistry Au. Computational cost is low, since no field ramps are required.
299: 
300: \subsection{Study 3 (satellite): memory of the \emph{director} after a pulse}
301: 
302: \textbf{Question.} After a saturating field pulse, does the \emph{director} (emergent preferred direction, concept from the 1998 work~\cite{prb1998}) formed during relaxation retain memory of the pulse direction, or is it erased and redetermined by the initial noise? Equilibrium Monte Carlo cannot answer this, since it requires a real time trajectory.
303: 
304: \begin{enumerate}
305: \item Pulse mode with field direction $\varphi$ swept, for example from $0^\circ$ to $90^\circ$ in $15^\circ$ steps, several seeds, and 2--3 $Q$ values;
306: \item Measure the direction of the final \emph{director} (mean orientation of the main domains) as a function of $\varphi$;
307: \item Quantify the directional correlation $\langle \cos 2(\theta_{\mathrm{dir}} - \varphi) \rangle$ and its dependence on $Q$. The interesting hypothesis is that memory is stronger in the overdamped regime, without oscillations that ``shake'' the system out of the valley selected by the pulse.
308: \end{enumerate}
309: 
310: \textbf{Destination:} connects to the recent literature on \emph{clocked dynamics} and training in \emph{artificial spin ice}; a good candidate for a second article. Moderate cost.
311: 
312: \subsection{Study 4 (satellite): boundary conditions and finite size in the domain scale}
313: 
314: \textbf{Question.} How much does the free boundary distort domain and avalanche statistics relative to the periodic limit? Long-range dipolar systems are notoriously sensitive to sample shape; the answer is methodologically useful for the whole community simulating these arrays without PBC.
315: 
316: \begin{enumerate}
317: \item Sweep $N \in \{14, 20, 30, 40\}$ with and without PBC, at $Q \approx 1$ and at the two extremes;
318: \item Repeat domain and avalanche analyses; extrapolate the characteristic domain scale with $1/N$;
319: \item Quantify the systematic free-boundary vs.\ periodic deviation.
320: \end{enumerate}
321: 
322: \textbf{Destination:} appendix/methods section of the central article, or an independent methodological note. The $N = 40$ point without optimizations is expensive ($\mathcal{O}(K^2)$ with $K = 1600$), making this study the main motivation for the performance optimization proposed in Section~\ref{sec:melhorias}.
323: 
324: % ======================================================================
325: \section{Proposed code improvements}
326: \label{sec:melhorias}
327: % ======================================================================
328: 
329: Analysis of the V39 code identified the following opportunities, ordered by estimated impact. None blocks Study~1, which is already feasible; the performance items make Studies~2--4 substantially cheaper.
330: 
331: \subsection{Performance}
332: 
333: \begin{enumerate}
334: \item \textbf{Precomputation of the dipolar-interaction tensor --- IMPLEMENTED (V40, 04/07).} Needle positions are fixed and only the angles evolve, but up to V39 the $K \times K$ geometry matrices (displacements, distances, powers of $r$, \emph{cutoff} masks, and PBC image sums) were rebuilt at every integration step. Because the dipolar field is \emph{linear} in the moments $(m_x, m_y)$, all geometry was condensed once before the loop into three constant matrices $A_{xx}, A_{xy}, A_{yy}$ ($A_{yx} = A_{xy}$; periodic images already summed), reducing each step to matrix--vector products via BLAS/cuBLAS.
335: 
336: \emph{Validation} (three levels): tensor torques vs. stepwise method identical to machine \emph{epsilon} (maximum relative error $3 \times 10^{-16}$, for the 3 geometries, with and without PBC); full hysteresis trajectory with 172 steps and bit-identical $t$ and $B$ relative to V39, with $M$ and $S$ agreeing within $5 \times 10^{-12}$ (expected accumulation from floating-point reassociation); all 4 field modes and \mbox{Ctrl+C} behavior (code 130) preserved.
337: 
338: \emph{Measured result} (array $30\times30$, same baseline parameters): from 117.8~s / 148.0~s per hysteresis run to \textbf{3.4~s / 3.3~s}, a \textbf{35--45$\times$} speedup, above the initial 5--20$\times$ estimate. The tensor occupies only 19~MB for $K = 900$. Built-in memory protection: above an estimated 4~GB (K~$\gtrsim$~13,000), the code reports the issue and automatically falls back to the per-step method. The full campaign dropped from $\sim$7--8~h to $\sim$15~min serial;
339: \item \textbf{Campaign parallelization --- IMPLEMENTED (\code{damping\_sweep} V02, 04/07).} Three features: \code{--n\_workers~N} runs independent processes (each worker in its own working directory, avoiding collisions among incidental files written by the simulator); \textbf{incremental} \emph{manifest}, updated after each completed run (the previous version wrote it only at the end, so an interruption lost the whole record, a vulnerability that appeared during tests); and \code{--resume~1}, which resumes an interrupted campaign by skipping already registered runs.
340: 
341: \emph{Validation}: parallel execution with 4 \emph{workers} produces CSVs bit-identical to serial execution; full resume (``nothing to do'') and partial resume (simulated interruption) work; the analysis script reads the V02 \emph{manifest} without changes. The time gain is expected to be approximately linear in the number of cores, not measurable in the one-core development environment and to be confirmed on the server;
342: \item \textbf{Optional single precision on GPU (proposed).} \code{float32} doubles memory throughput; it should be sufficient for avalanche statistics, but must be validated against \code{float64} in one case before adoption;
343: \item \textbf{Adaptive time step in the overdamped regime (proposed; investigate cautiously).} With $Q \ll 1$, the dynamics is slow and $\Delta t$ tied to $T_0$ is conservative. A larger step, limited by the viscous scale, could reduce the cost of the slowest runs. This requires a dedicated convergence study before adoption. With the V40 gain, it is no longer a priority.
344: \end{enumerate}
345: 
346: Two relevant optimizations are already implemented and should not be redone: vectorized rendering (17--30$\times$, V35) and NVENC video encoding when \code{--gpu 1}.
347: 
348: \subsection{Visual appearance}
349: 
350: \begin{enumerate}
351: \item \textbf{Publication-figure mode} (\code{--fig\_style paper}): white background, colorblind-safe palette, vector export (PDF/SVG), fonts sized for article columns, and no HUD elements (clock, field indicator). Current figures (dark theme, PNG) are excellent for screen/video, but unsuitable for the manuscript. This is the highest immediate-value visual improvement for publication;
352: \item \textbf{Live $M\times H$ curve in the hysteresis video --- IMPLEMENTED (V45).} The \code{hysteresis} video now has a dual panel: needles on the left and the $M\times H$ curve on the right, traced in real time with the current point highlighted, communicating the full experiment in a single video. Other video modes remain single-panel; physics is bit-identical, since this is a rendering-only change;
353: \item \textbf{Visual highlighting of avalanches}: emphasize needles that rotated above a threshold in the last $n$ steps, showing the spatial propagation of avalanches and connecting the video to the paper statistics;
354: \item \textbf{Domain-wall drawing}: draw edges between domains with distinct labels, complementing colored halos and improving readability in large arrays;
355: \item \textbf{Net-magnetization arrow}: global vector $\vec M$ overlaid on the array, with instantaneous magnitude and direction;
356: \item \textbf{Energy halo mode}: heat map of dipolar energy per needle, revealing local frustration and likely avalanche nuclei.
357: \end{enumerate}
358: 
359: % ======================================================================
360: \section{Next steps}
361: % ======================================================================
362: 
363: \begin{enumerate}
364: \item Run the full campaign on the server (command documented in the scripts; \code{--quick\_test 1} is recommended first to validate the environment);
365: \item Run the analysis and apply the A/B criterion to $\tau(Q)$ with full statistics; in particular, check whether the slope of $\tau(Q)$ differs between geometries and whether the triangular-domain non-monotonicity survives;
366: \item Robustness diagnostics before any conclusion: convergence in $\Delta t$ at the extremes of $Q$; finite-size effects ($N = 20$ and $40$ at $Q \approx 1$); comparison with/without PBC;
367: \item Choose the journal based on the result (A $\to$ ACS Materials Au / Physical Chemistry Au; B $\to$ consider PRL) and start the manuscript framed as a ``model for \emph{artificial spin ice} and patterned nanomagnets'';
368: \item Satellite studies from the original roadmap, according to capacity: domain-formation kinetics by geometry; memory of the ``director'' after a pulse; effect of PBC on domain scale.
369: \end{enumerate}
370: 
371: % ======================================================================
372: \section{Version record}
373: % ======================================================================
374: 
375: \begin{longtable}{@{}lp{11.5cm}@{}}
376: \caption{Version record for \code{compass\_sim}. Versions V1--V19 precede the formal record and were consolidated retroactively; from V20 onward each change generates a new numbered version.}
377: \label{tab:versoes} \\
378: \toprule
379: \textbf{Version} & \textbf{Main content} \\
380: \midrule
381: \endfirsthead
382: \toprule
383: \textbf{Version} & \textbf{Main content} \\
384: \midrule
385: \endhead
386: \bottomrule
387: \endfoot
388: \bottomrule
389: \endlastfoot
390: V1--V19 & Simulator core: inertial dipolar dynamics (SI), three geometries, external field, MP4 video, field modes (static/pulse/hysteresis/sine), CSV export, PBC, and corrections to honeycomb and \code{t\_sim} semantics. \\
391: V20 & Formal version-record milestone. \\
392: V21--V24 & Pulse-mode refinement ($S$ plateau criterion); repositioned on-screen indicators (field at upper right, dedicated upper margin). \\
393: V25--V28 & GPU support via CuPy; \code{--gpu} flag; utilization investigation; performance instrumentation (throughput, ms/step). \\
394: V29--V33 & PBC with structure images in the dipolar field (default one copy); progress bar; automatic moment of inertia from real geometry (\SI{0.4}{mm} steel); Ctrl+C aborts immediately / Ctrl+I saves and exits (code 130). \\
395: V34 & Consolidation and corrections of GPU vectorization of the torque loop. \\
